## What was built

**`test/dao/stream/slice_peer.cljc` (186 lines)** — process B as a whole program, not a fixture. It receives A's descriptor as Transit JSON **text in `argv`** (the bootstrap channel, decoded through `transit/decode-descriptor`), and speaks a line-oriented Transit command/reply protocol on stdin/stdout. Its deposit medium and `:dao.stream/newest` cursor are minted **once, before the first `attach!`**, and survive every attachment. `#?@(:cljd [] :clj […] :cljs […])` with `:cljd` first, per the reader-conditional trap.

**`test/dao/stream/slice_test.clj` (544 lines)** — process A on the JVM. One ring buffer behind two `ws/make-endpoint` + `serving/make-serving` compositions over `jvm/listen!` (port 0, four handoff slots each), one driver thread. Peers are spawned with `ProcessBuilder` on the live classpath (`java -cp … clojure.main -m …`), which avoids a second dependency resolution. A `:once` fixture shares process A across all five facts — a per-test server would make fact 2's "the served stream survives" unfalsifiable.

**`test/dao/stream/slice_test.cljs` (579 lines)** — process A on Node. Same five facts; peers are real `child_process.spawn`ed `node target/slice-peer.js` processes; every wait is a promise polling the event loop.

**`shadow-cljs.edn`** — `:slice-peer` `:node-script` build. **`bb.edn`** — `test:cljs` now runs `compile slice-peer test` so the peer artifact exists before `:autorun`.

## The five facts and where each is asserted

| | Fact | On A | On B |
|---|---|---|---|
|1| detachment observable both sides | per-attachment medium ends `:ws/closed`; session gone from `serving/state`; `append!` to `served` still `ok` | `append!` after close → `:dao.stream/closed`; own medium deposits `:ws/closed` |
|2| same descriptor rejoins same logical stream | append to the surviving buffer | reattach with the identical bootstrapped value → `ok`, new attachment id, receives the append |
|3| distinct `:dao.stream/attachment` | two server media, every event carries its own id | two processes, distinct ids, every event on each medium carries its own |
|4| B's cursor outlives the socket | — | `declared-surfaces` = `#{:writer :closable}` (no reader surface); the kept cursor's walk is exactly `[:ws/opened :ws/payload :ws/closed]`, in order, no repeats |
|5| two endpoints, one identity | descriptors differ in reachability, identity equal; a `served` cursor works without either descriptor | both handles project distinct descriptors and the same `:dao.stream/identity`; both receive the same append |

